# What the Lima guest adds to the WSL guest's packages (vm/wsl/packages, read
# from there so the two guests run the same container stack): what a virtual
# machine of its own boots with, which WSL provides itself, and what Lima
# needs inside the guest. The kernel (linux-image-cloud-<arch>) is added by
# mkimage.sh. Versions come from the same pinned snapshot; Recommends are not
# installed.
#
# Booting: the kernel's initrd and device manager, and systemd-boot.
udev
kmod
initramfs-tools
zstd
systemd-boot-efi
e2fsprogs
# Networking, DNS and the clock, which WSL sets up from outside.
systemd-resolved
systemd-timesyncd
# Lima: its settings arrive through cloud-init from a small disk it attaches;
# cloud-init grows the root partition to the VM's disk size (growpart needs
# sfdisk); Lima reaches the guest over SSH and runs its boot scripts with
# sudo; it installs rsync at boot when it is missing, so it is here instead.
cloud-init
cloud-guest-utils
fdisk
openssh-server
sudo
# The login session SSH opens for Lima's user, with its systemd user manager.
libpam-systemd
rsync
procps
